./LOGBOOK.md:112:- ROOT CAUSE: The real-sshd cancellation test opened two `sleep 2` exec channels while injecting a `ManualFixtureClock`. Caller cancellation correctly detached the pending read, but the following channel disposal still had to advance libssh2 cleanup behind the bridge-owned POSIX service and remote process exit. Because the manual clock also owned the channel-close deadline and was no longer advanced, cleanup could not expire independently; suite completion depended on two wall-clock subprocess lifetimes and could hang when socket/sshd retirement lost the favorable scheduling race. A clean baseline measured 4.116 seconds for this test, matching the two sequential sleeps.
./LOGBOOK.md:231:- CONFORMANCE: A connected OpenSSH regression starts with both automatic schedulers active, holds socket close past the 50 ms transport deadline while ignoring cancellation, proves bounded close, exact-once socket close, repeated-close safety, detached ownership visibility, and eventual zero sessions/channels/sockets/tasks/allocations/buffers after release. The formerly intermittent repeated-handshake cleanup passed 10 consecutive focused runs.
./LOGBOOK.md:249:- REWORK: Timeout races now detach callers promptly while a bounded registry retains, cancels, and reports unfinished injected work until it actually returns. Upload keeps the libssh2 channel state machine actor-owned and applies one absolute deadline across open, source read, bounded drains, writes, EOF, and exit; only the injected source read is detached. External signer work follows the same visible ownership rule instead of making teardown wait indefinitely.
